2.1 Master switch: ‘bookmark-gt-mode’
=====================================

Global minor mode.  Enables every bookmark-gt feature; when off, no
advice or hook is installed and the built-in ‘bookmark.el’ operates
unmodified.

   When enabled:

   • *Set operations.*  ‘bookmark-gt-set’ reads tags, applies the
     same-name policy, and assigns bookmark-gt's Dired handler when the
     current buffer is Dired.  Its name prompt starts out holding the
     suggested name — derived from the location being bookmarked, not
     from the last bookmark jumped to — as editable text: ‘RET’ accepts
     it, point is at its end for a small edit, and ‘C-a C-k’ replaces
     it.  Names matching ‘bookmark-gt-auto-temp-names’ (e.g.
     ‘org-capture-last-stored’) are marked temporary at store time,
     which excludes them from ‘bookmark-save’ output.
   • *Jump operations.*  Every jump increments the record's ‘visits’
     property and updates ‘last-visited’.  File bookmarks display as
     line overlays in their visited buffers.  Region bookmarks reselect
     their region on jump.  URL and browser-tab handlers suppress the
     built-in annotation-buffer display.
   • *Jump reader.*  ‘bookmark-gt-jump’ is the recommended replacement
     for the built-in ‘bookmark-jump’.  It sorts candidates MRU-first
     using the ‘visits’ / ‘last-visited’ values above, integrates with
     ‘marginalia’ annotations and the ‘consult’ / ‘orderless’ narrowing
     particles (‘,@TYPE’ / ‘;TAG’) when those packages are loaded, and
     supports a mid-read tag filter (‘M-t’ adds, ‘M-d’ pops, ‘M-D’
     clears).
   • *File-type dispatch.*  File bookmarks whose ‘filename’ matches a
     regexp in ‘bookmark-gt-file-type-handlers’ are handled by the
     associated function instead of the built-in ‘find-file’ path.
   • *File-rename tracking.*  ‘rename-file’ (including from Dired's ‘R’)
     rewrites every bookmark whose ‘filename’ equals the source path to
     the destination.
   • *Temporary bookmarks.*  A bookmark marked temporary exists for the
     duration of the Emacs session.  It appears in the list buffer and
     the jump reader like any other bookmark, but it is never written to
     the bookmark file, and it never marks the bookmark file as out of
     date: adding temporary bookmarks, or refreshing the browser-tab
     list, causes neither an auto-save nor a write when you quit Emacs.
     Making a temporary bookmark permanent, or a permanent one
     temporary, changes what a save would write and is recorded as an
     edit like any other.

   Disabling the mode removes every advice and hook that enabling
installed.  The three opt-in modes described below (auto-update,
default-tags, browser-tabs) each add further behavior and are controlled
independently.


