2.2.1 ‘bookmark-gt-auto-update-mode’
------------------------------------

Bookmarks carrying the ‘auto-update’ alist property get their position
refreshed to the visiting buffer's current point on every idle tick.
Useful for e-readers, long-form documents, ‘pdf-tools’ page tracking.

   Triggers: an idle timer (‘bookmark-gt-auto-update-interval’, default
60 s), plus ‘kill-buffer-hook’ and ‘window-state-change-hook’ to capture
buffer closes and window switches without waiting for the next idle
tick.  Pressing ‘g’ in the list buffer also triggers a refresh before
the buffer redraws.

   Toggle the property with ‘^’ in the list buffer — on the marked rows,
or on the row at point when none are marked — or with ‘M-x
bookmark-gt-auto-update-toggle NAME’.  Set it programmatically with
‘bookmark-gt-auto-update-set’.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: bookmark-gt-default-tags-mode,  Next: bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-mode,  Prev: bookmark-gt-auto-update-mode,  Up: Opt-in modes

2.2.2 ‘bookmark-gt-default-tags-mode’
-------------------------------------

Seeds the tag reader on ‘bookmark-gt-set’ with context-appropriate tags.
Configured via a small DSL:

     (setq bookmark-gt-default-tags "work")                    ; single tag
     (setq bookmark-gt-default-tags '("work" "urgent"))        ; list
     (setq bookmark-gt-default-tags                            ; function
           (lambda (record)
             (cond ((string-prefix-p "/proj/" (bookmark-gt-filename-of record))
                    '("project"))
                   ((eq (bookmark-gt-handler-type record) 'url)
                    '("web"))
                   (t nil))))

   Errors in the function form are demoted to a warning; nothing is
applied without user confirmation.  The user always sees the defaults as
prefill in the tag reader and can edit or clear them.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-mode,  Prev: bookmark-gt-default-tags-mode,  Up: Opt-in modes

2.2.3 ‘bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-mode’
-------------------------------------

Exposes the browser's open tabs as bookmarks (temporary — not saved to
disk).  Requires ‘browser-gt’ to be installed and connected.

   Making a tab bookmark permanent (‘T’ in the list buffer) converts it
into a plain URL bookmark, saved like any other.  The tab is still open,
so the next refresh adds it back to the list as a temporary bookmark.

   Refresh triggers:

   • Every ‘bookmark-gt-jump’ call.  The mode adds
     ‘bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-refresh’ to
     ‘bookmark-gt-jump-before-read-hook’, so the reader sees current
     tabs at each read.  Tab state changes faster than connect and
     disconnect events fire, and one fetch per jump is cheaper than a
     polling timer.
   • Browser connect and disconnect events.  The mode subscribes to
     ‘browser-gt-client-connected-functions’ and
     ‘browser-gt-client-disconnected-functions’ and refreshes the tab
     records whenever a browser attaches or detaches.  Bursts of events
     within ‘bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-debounce’ seconds (default 0.3)
     are coalesced into a single refresh.
   • Mode enable: one refresh runs immediately when a browser is already
     connected.  When no browser is connected, the first refresh runs on
     the next connect event or the next jump.
   • ‘g’ (revert) in the ‘*Bookmarks-gt List*’ buffer.
   • ‘M-x bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-refresh’ for a manual refresh.

   Turning the mode off removes the connect and disconnect hooks and the
jump-before-read hook, cancels any pending debounced refresh, and clears
the tab records added by this mode.  Records owned by other
browser-gt-related packages (‘browser-gt-tab-manager-bookmark-jump’
etc.)  are left alone.

   Deleting a browser-tab bookmark from the ‘*Bookmarks-gt List*’ buffer
removes only the bookmark record; the browser tab itself stays open and
the next refresh will re-add the record.  Closing the tab in the browser
is a separate operation.  This matches the rest of bookmark-gt: deleting
a bookmark never touches the resource it points to (no file is deleted,
no buffer is killed, no URL is un-visited).

   A full refresh runs as one batch: no tag prompt per tab, no auto-save
while it runs, and no pending write when it finishes.  Tab records are
temporary, so a refresh — however many tabs it stores — never writes the
bookmark file.

   Each stored tab record's ‘last-visited’ is seeded from the tab's
‘:lastAccessed’ field (Chrome / Firefox report this as the last time the
tab was focused, in milliseconds since epoch).  MRU sort in
‘bookmark-gt-jump’ therefore orders tabs by the browser's own
last-focused ordering, and every pre-jump refresh re-reads the field so
the ordering tracks browser activity rather than bookmark-gt jump
history.

   Customizable:

   • ‘bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-browsers’ — list of browser clients to
     query, nil = all connected.
   • ‘bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-filter’ — nil / regexp / predicate
     applied per tab before storing.
   • ‘bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-debounce’ — seconds to coalesce browser
     connect and disconnect events before triggering a refresh (default
     0.3).


