3.3.1 URL bookmarks — ‘bookmark-gt-set-url’
-------------------------------------------

Interactive:

     M-x bookmark-gt-set-url RET
     URL: https://example.org RET
     Bookmark name: Example RET

   Elisp:

     (bookmark-gt-set-url "https://example.org" "Example" '("web" "reference"))

   The URL bookmark's handler (‘bookmark-gt-handler-url-jump’) reads the
‘url’ property off the record and opens it with ‘browse-url’, so URL
bookmarks follow whatever ‘browse-url-browser-function’ and
‘browse-url-handlers’ you have configured — the same routing as every
other link you open in Emacs.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Dired bookmarks — bookmark-gt-handler-dired-jump,  Next: Function bookmarks — bookmark-gt-set-function,  Prev: URL bookmarks — bookmark-gt-set-url,  Up: Non-file bookmark types

3.3.2 Dired bookmarks — ‘bookmark-gt-handler-dired-jump’
--------------------------------------------------------

The built-in ‘dired.el’ does not provide a bookmark handler.
bookmark-gt provides one that opens the recorded target and restores the
buffer state.  The setter captures, and the jump handler restores:

   • ‘dired-directory’ — the original value, so plain paths, wildcard
     patterns (e.g.  ‘/tmp/*.el’), and explicit file lists round-trip
     verbatim;
   • ‘dired-marked’ — files marked in the buffer, keyed by mark
     character (uses ‘dired-remember-marks’ / ‘dired-mark-remembered’ —
     the same primitives bookmark+ uses);
   • ‘dired-subdirs’ — subdirectories inserted with ‘i’;
   • ‘dired-hidden-dirs’ — subdirectories currently hidden (‘$’);
   • ‘dired-switches’ — ‘dired-actual-switches’ in effect.

   The alist keys match bookmark+'s (‘bmkp-jump-dired’), so records
interchange on disk in either direction: a Dired bookmark written by
bookmark+ restores under bookmark-gt with all its state, and vice-versa.
Records with the bookmark+ handler symbol classify as Dired via the
registry alias; you can also convert them to bookmark-gt form with ‘M-x
bookmark-gt-migrate-from-bookmark-plus’ (see *note Migrating from
bookmark+::).

  1. Virtual dired

     Setting a bookmark from a virtual-dired buffer (‘M-x dired-virtual’
     or the ‘V’ command in ‘dired-x’) additionally inlines the buffer's
     textual listing under a ‘dired-listing’ key, and marks the record
     with ‘dired-virtual t’.  On jump, the handler creates a fresh
     buffer, inserts the listing, and calls ‘dired-virtual’ to
     reconstruct the same view — even if the source directory no longer
     exists.

     This is not a bookmark+ compatibility feature: bookmark+ does not
     preserve virtual-dired contents and re-opens the recorded directory
     as real dired.  The bookmark-gt behavior is opt-in via the buffer
     being virtual; you do nothing special to set one.

     The listing is bounded by ‘bookmark-gt-dired-virtual-max-size’
     (bytes; default 524288 = 512 KB). Setting a larger virtual-dired
     buffer signals ‘user-error’ — raise the cap in the defcustom, or
     bookmark a smaller portion.  The rationale for the cap: the
     bookmark file is loaded whole at startup and rewritten whole on
     every save, so an inlined multi-megabyte listing multiplies that
     cost on every unrelated bookmark change.


