4.1 Tag reader
==============

‘bookmark-gt-tags-read’ prompts for tags one at a time.  Each prompt is
a plain ‘completing-read’ against every tag that currently appears in
‘bookmark-alist’, ordered most-recently-used first (based on
‘bookmark-gt-tags-history’; completion history is the MRU signal).

   Terminators:
   • Empty ‘RET’ — finish, return the accumulated list.
   • ‘M-RET’ — submit the current input (if non-empty), then finish.

   Any non-empty ‘RET’ accepts the current tag and prompts for another.
The running accumulator is shown in the prompt as ~Tags [foo,bar] (M-RET
or empty RET to finish): ~.

   The reader is invoked by ‘bookmark-gt-set’ when
‘bookmark-gt-prompt-for-tags-flag’ is non-nil (default) and seeds itself
with any tags provided by ‘bookmark-gt-default-tags-mode’ or another
‘bookmark-gt-set-tag-reader-hook’ contributor.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Same-name policy,  Next: Relocate change a bookmark's target,  Prev: Tag reader,  Up: Setting bookmarks

4.2 Same-name policy
====================

Setting a bookmark whose name is already in use is not an error.  Two
defcustoms determine the result, and each applies to a different kind of
collision — for any single ‘bookmark-gt-set’ call at most one of them is
consulted:

   • ‘bookmark-gt-same-name-overwrite’ applies to a *full collision*:
     the existing record has the same name AND the same handler AND the
     same filename.  The new record is then the same bookmark at a new
     position.
   • ‘bookmark-gt-allow-duplicate-names’ applies to a *partial
     collision*: the existing record has the same name but a different
     filename or a different handler.  The new record is then a
     different bookmark that reuses an existing name.

   The name the new record ends up stored under is determined by these
rules, in order:

  1. *Prefix-argument override.*  When ‘M-x bookmark-gt-set’ is called
     with a prefix argument (‘NO-OVERWRITE’ non-nil from Lisp), a ‘<N>’
     suffix is always appended.  Same semantics as built-in
     ‘bookmark-set’ with ‘C-u’.
  2. *Update in place.*  If ‘bookmark-gt-same-name-overwrite’ is non-nil
     (the default) and the existing record has the same handler AND the
     same filename as the new one, the existing record is replaced in
     place.  This is the "update this bookmark's location" case:
     re-setting ‘todo’ inside ‘todo.org’ when ‘todo’ already points to
     ‘todo.org’ moves the existing record's position to point.
  3. *Shared name across records.*  If
     ‘bookmark-gt-allow-duplicate-names’ is non-nil (the default), the
     new record is stored under the literal name even when another
     record already uses it — two bookmarks in different files (or with
     different handlers) can share the same name.  Example: a ‘todo’
     bookmark in each of several project files.
  4. *Fallback.*  With both flags off, any collision that isn't handled
     by rule 2 receives a ‘<N>’ suffix.

* Menu:

* Worked scenario the same name in two projects::
* If you expected two records and see one::
* Warning only the list buffer addresses duplicates::


