4.2.1 Worked scenario: the same name in two projects
----------------------------------------------------

Set a bookmark named ‘todo’ in ‘~/src/projA/notes.org’, then set another
named ‘todo’ in ‘~/src/projB/notes.org’.  The two records have the same
handler but different filenames, so this is a partial collision: rule 2
does not apply whatever the value of ‘bookmark-gt-same-name-overwrite’,
and rule 3 determines the result.

‘same-name-overwrite’   ‘allow-duplicate-names’   Result
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘t’ (default)           ‘t’ (default)             two records, both named ‘todo’
‘nil’                   ‘t’                       two records, both named ‘todo’
‘t’                     ‘nil’                     ‘todo’ and ‘todo<2>’
‘nil’                   ‘nil’                     ‘todo’ and ‘todo<2>’

   The left column has no effect here.  It would matter only if the
second ‘bookmark-gt-set’ ran in ‘projA/notes.org’ again — the
full-collision case, where ‘t’ moves the existing record's position to
point and ‘nil’ produces ‘todo<2>’.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: If you expected two records and see one,  Next: Warning only the list buffer addresses duplicates,  Prev: Worked scenario the same name in two projects,  Up: Same-name policy

4.2.2 If you expected two records and see one
---------------------------------------------

The rules above describe what is stored.  A record can be stored and
still be absent from the list buffer, so there are two possible causes.
Count the stored records to determine which one applies:

     (seq-filter (lambda (r)
                   (equal (substring-no-properties (car r)) "todo"))
                 bookmark-alist)

   *Two records.*  Both were stored; the list buffer is not displaying
one of them.  Either:

   • *A filter is active.*  Filters on tags, group, or type restrict
     which records ‘bookmark-gt-list’ prints; ‘bookmark-alist’ is
     unmodified.  Filters persist across sessions via
     ‘bookmark-gt-list-state-file’, so a filter set in an earlier
     session is still in effect.  ‘V’ resets the view to the defaults;
     ‘/’ sets a filter.
   • *One record is temporary and temporary records are hidden.*
     Temporary records are omitted from the display, not from
     ‘bookmark-alist’, and they remain reachable by ‘bookmark-gt-jump’.
     ‘H’ toggles their visibility.  (‘T’ changes whether the record at
     point is temporary, which modifies the record — it is not a display
     toggle.)

   *One record.*  Only one was stored under that name.  Check whether a
‘todo<2>’ record exists: if it does, rule 1 or rule 4 applied and the
second bookmark was stored under the disambiguated name.  If it does
not, rule 2 applied and the second ‘bookmark-gt-set’ replaced the first,
which means the two filenames refer to the same file.  Rule 2 compares
them with ‘string=’ and, when both files exist, with ‘file-equal-p’ — so
two paths that differ only by a symlink are a full collision.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Warning only the list buffer addresses duplicates,  Prev: If you expected two records and see one,  Up: Same-name policy

4.2.3 Warning: only the list buffer addresses duplicates
--------------------------------------------------------

Rule 3 has one consequence, and it is severe enough to decide whether
you want duplicate names at all.

   A bookmark name is not an identifier.  ‘bookmark-get-bookmark’
resolves a name with ‘assoc’ and returns the *first* record carrying it.
Every command that takes a name inherits this, so when two records share
a name only one of them is reachable by name — and which one is an
accident of the order records sit in ‘bookmark-alist’.

   *Affected — do not use these on a duplicated name:*

Command                     Effect on a duplicated name
-----------------------------------------------------------------
‘bookmark-delete’           deletes an arbitrary one of them
‘bookmark-rename’           renames an arbitrary one of them
‘bookmark-relocate’         retargets an arbitrary one of them
‘bookmark-jump’             always jumps to the first
‘bookmark-insert’           inserts the first
‘bookmark-gt-relocate’      retargets the first
‘bookmark-gt-toggle-temp’   acts on the first

   ‘bookmark-delete’ and ‘bookmark-rename’ are the dangerous entries:
they modify or destroy a record you did not choose, without a
diagnostic.  Emacs's own ‘bookmark-delete’ docstring states that it
"removes only the first instance of a bookmark with that name".

   *‘bookmark-gt-jump’ is also affected.*  It lists every record
separately — both duplicates appear as candidates, annotated with their
own tags and location — but it returns the selected candidate's *name*,
and the jump then resolves that name to the first match.  Selecting the
second of two duplicates jumps to the first.

   *The list buffer distinguishes duplicates on screen but does not
consistently act on the row you chose.*  Each row holds its own record,
and the Location and Tags columns show them apart.  Commands differ in
whether they keep that record:

List command                            Key     Acts on
--------------------------------------------------------------------
‘bookmark-gt-list-edit-tags’ etc.       ‘t e’   the row (correct)
‘bookmark-gt-list-toggle-temp’          ‘T’     the row (correct)
‘bookmark-gt-list-toggle-auto-update’   ‘^’     the row (correct)
‘bookmark-gt-list-jump’                 ‘RET’   the *first* match
‘bookmark-gt-list-relocate’             ‘R’     the *first* match
‘bookmark-gt-list-rename’               ‘r’     the *first* match
‘bookmark-gt-list-execute-deletions’    ‘x’     the *first* match

   The last four resolve the row's record back to its name before
acting, so they inherit first-match resolution.  Deletion is the
consequential one: flagging the second of two duplicates and pressing
‘x’ deletes the first record and leaves the flagged row in place.

   *Recommendation.*  Until this is fixed, treat duplicate names as safe
only for records you never need to delete, rename, relocate, or jump to
individually.  If you need to act on specific records, set
‘bookmark-gt-allow-duplicate-names’ to nil so collisions get a ‘<N>’
suffix and every name stays addressable.

   This is a known defect, not a design intent.  Bookmark names were
never identifiers; the fix is to give records a stable id and resolve by
record rather than by name.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Relocate change a bookmark's target,  Prev: Same-name policy,  Up: Setting bookmarks

4.3 Relocate: change a bookmark's target
========================================

‘M-x bookmark-gt-relocate NAME’ changes where an existing bookmark
points, dispatching on the record's shape:

   • File records (a ‘filename’ key) prompt via ‘read-file-name’;
     default is the current filename so a rename is a matter of editing.
     ‘position’ is left unchanged — the common trigger is a file rename,
     and the offset is still valid.
   • URL records (a ‘url’ key: URL bookmarks, browser-tab bookmarks)
     prompt for the new URL via ‘read-string’.
   • Records with neither key raise a ‘user-error’.

   In the list buffer, ‘R’ relocates the row's record.  The after-hook
fires so the list, highlight overlays, and any other observer refresh
automatically.

   For a genuinely new position within a different file, jump to the
target, place point, and re-invoke ‘bookmark-gt-set’ with the same name
— the same-name-overwrite policy (above) updates ‘position’ in place.


