10 Acknowledgements
*******************

bookmark-gt owes a substantial debt to Drew Adams
(https://www.emacswiki.org/emacs/DrewsElispLibraries) and his bookmark+
(https://www.emacswiki.org/emacs/BookmarkPlus) package, both in design
and in code.  Most of the features bookmark-gt provides — tags, non-file
bookmark types (URL, EWW, region, function, sequence, kmacro), temporary
bookmarks, visit tracking, per-bookmark highlighting, and tag-driven
filtering — were first introduced in bookmark+.

   Concretely reused:

   • *Handler bodies.*  The URL bookmark handler
     (‘bookmark-gt-handler-url-jump’) and the Dired handler
     (‘bookmark-gt-handler-dired-jump’) were copied from bookmark+'s
     corresponding handlers (‘bmkp-jump-url-browse’ and
     ‘bmkp-jump-dired’) and lightly adapted — same jump semantics, same
     record-shape expectations, minor changes to fit bookmark-gt's
     registry.
   • *Record schema.*  The on-disk alist keys — ‘tags’, ‘bmkp-temp’,
     ‘auto-update’, ‘visits’, ‘last-visited’, ‘location’ (URL alias),
     and ‘front-context-region-string’ / ‘rear-context-region-string’
     (region bookmarks) — are adopted from bookmark+'s format verbatim:
     bookmark-gt reads and writes them under the same names, so a
     bookmark file moves between the two packages without losing these
     fields.

     One item of that format is read but not written: the ‘" - no file
     -"’ string bookmark+ stores in ‘filename’ on a non-file record.
     Bookmark-gt omits ‘filename’ on such records instead, and
     ‘bookmark-gt-filename-of’ reads the placeholder as absent (see
     *note Data compat with bookmark+::).  Whether bookmark+ in turn
     accepts a record with no ‘filename’ key has not been tested —
     bookmark-gt has no dependency on it and the test suite does not
     install it.

   ‘bookmark-gt’ is a rewrite with different architectural choices (no
advice on other packages, a single registry per axis,
tabulated-list-mode instead of custom rendering), not a fork.  But the
user-visible features, the design ideas, and the handler code included
in this package all originate from Drew's work.


